From a38e44d93083914d53d3529c04d4a60a1ead2ec1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LUgABeEEE <LUgABeEEE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:13:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(android): proceed pinned WebSocket certificate errors

Chromium reports a WebSocket handshake's certificate error to
WebViewClient.onReceivedSslError with a `wss:` URL. The pinned check compared
that URL against the page origin with GatewayUrlPolicy.isSameOrigin, which only
accepts `https:`, so a paired page's own /api/remote.mux channel was treated as
a foreign identity: the handler cancelled it, failed the document and
stopLoading()ed, and the user saw "安全连接失败 / secure connection failed" over a
page that never finished booting. The certificate itself is fine — issuer,
signature, validity and SAN all verify; only the scheme differs.

Accept the secure scheme family (https/wss) for certificate decisions while
keeping host, port and the leaf pin exact. A cleartext URL, another port, another
host or a URL carrying credentials still cancels.

Trigger is timing-dependent, not device-specific: the failure is reproducible on
a Xiaomi Pad 9 Pro Max (Android 17, WebView 150.0.7871.181) when the document's
boot batches are served quickly enough that the handshake reaches the callback
before the page's certificate decision covers it (we hit it while measuring a
local boot-batch cache, not included here); on the stock network path on the
same device the handshake lands later and we did not observe the failure. The
check is wrong either way: a `wss:` URL can never satisfy an `https:`-only
comparison.

70 suites / 454 tests pass, :app:lintDebug reports 0 errors, and the new
GatewayUrlPolicyWebSocketTest pins the accepted and rejected schemes.

How it was found: the symptom started as an intermittent "secure connection
failed" surface with repeated net_error -202, while the native pinned probe and
renewal kept working (the gateway's device record kept advancing). CA validity,
leaf validity/SAN, device clock, proxy/VPN routing, the stored pin and a
release-to-release source diff were eliminated one by one; a throwaway debug
build then logged url, primaryError, the origin comparison and every pin
sub-check inside onReceivedSslError, and showed the rejected request was the
page's own wss:// stream with every certificate check true. Making the document
boot fast (a local boot-batch cache experiment, not part of this change) turned
it into a 3/3 reproduction; on the stock network path on the same device the
handshake lands later and the failure was not observed.

